We launched the Appwrite Education program to give students Pro-level Cloud resources while they learned to build. Verified members of the [GitHub Student Developer Pack](https://education.github.com/pack) could enroll, link their GitHub account, and receive an Education organization with two projects at Pro usage limits. It was a genuine attempt to help the next generation of developers get started without cost getting in the way.

**On November 1st, 2026, we are sunsetting the Education program.** New sign-ups will close, and existing Education organizations will move off the plan.

This post explains why we made that call, what it means if you are on the Education plan today, and how we will help you transition.

# What is changing on November 1st

Starting **November 1st, 2026**:

- The Appwrite Education program will no longer accept new members.
- Existing Education plan organizations will be transitioned off the program.
- Pro-level resources tied to the Education plan will no longer be available through GitHub Student Developer Pack verification.

If you are a verified student on the Education plan today, we will reach out with clear steps before November 1st. You will be able to move your projects to the **free plan**, upgrade to **Pro**, or run **self-hosted Appwrite** on infrastructure you control.

# Why we are sunsetting the Education program

We built the Education program with good intentions. Verified GitHub Student Developer Pack members could create two projects with usage limits aligned to our Pro plan, minus email support. For students building coursework, side projects, and hackathon apps, that was meant to remove friction.

Over time, the program stopped delivering the outcome we wanted.

## Misuse outweighed the help we could provide

We noticed that a significant share of Education plan accounts did not follow the program terms. Some ran commercial workloads. Some used the higher limits to host abusive traffic, spam infrastructure, and other activity that violated our policies.

That abuse was not an edge case. When we traced the worst incidents on Appwrite Cloud, a disproportionate share traced back to Education program accounts. The reputation damage and operational load did not come from students finishing homework. It came from people treating a student benefit like an anonymous Pro trial.

We tried to fix this with moderation, guardrails, and manual review. Accounts got suspended. Limits got tightened. Support tickets piled up from both abuse reports and students caught in the crossfire.

That work pulled engineers and support staff away from the product. Hours that should have gone into databases, auth, sites, and reliability went into chasing bad actors who had entered through a door we opened for students. We ended up investing in containment instead of making the platform better and growing the business.

## Free plan abuse exists, but at a smaller scale

Abuse also shows up on the free plan. That is expected on any platform with a no-cost tier.

The difference is scale and cost. The Education plan carried **Pro-level limits** on two projects. A bad actor on Education could do more damage, for longer, before limits or review caught up. Free plan limits are tighter and shared across projects, which kept most incidents smaller and easier to contain.

We still invest in abuse prevention on every plan. Ending the Education program removes the highest-leverage misuse path we had.

## Legitimate students were already close to free plan usage

Here is the part that surprised us.

We looked at Education organizations that **did** follow the terms: coursework, personal learning projects, no commercial use. Their usage patterns looked a lot like active free plan accounts. Most were not pushing Pro limits week after week. They were building real apps within bounds the free tier could reasonably support.

That told us something uncomfortable but clear. The program was creating a large abuse surface for a benefit that honest students rarely needed at Pro scale. Keeping it open meant keeping that surface open too.

# Putting that time back into Appwrite

The Education program kept engineering and support on verification disputes, manual review, suspensions, and abuse response. Those are the same teams that build Appwrite and run Cloud.

We are closing the program so they can spend that time on the product. That is how this decision makes Appwrite better.

# Thank you to GitHub and GitHub Education

The Education program ran in collaboration with the [GitHub Student Developer Pack](https://education.github.com/pack). That partnership made verification simple for real students, and we are grateful for it.

Thank you to the **GitHub team**, and especially the **GitHub Education team**, for years of support, feedback, and collaboration. They cared about getting students access to useful tools, and they worked with us in good faith when abuse created problems on both sides.

Appwrite has always been built for engineers and the open source community. Ending this program does not change that. We will keep looking for **different** ways to support students, contributors, and builders, without maintaining a tier that too many people treated as a loophole.

# How we will support your transition

If you are on the Education plan today, you do not need to figure this out alone.

Before November 1st, we will contact active Education organizations with:

- A timeline for your organization’s transition
- Guidance on moving projects to the [free plan](/pricing) or [Pro](/pricing)
- Options for [self-hosting](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you want full control on your own infrastructure
- Support if you hit blockers during migration

**Free plan.** Best if your usage matches what you were already running for coursework or personal projects. You keep building on Appwrite Cloud within free tier limits.

**Pro plan.** Best if you rely on Pro-level resources, need dedicated project limits, or run a project that outgrew free tier caps.

**Self-hosted.** Best if you want no Cloud usage caps beyond your own hardware, need a specific deployment environment, or prefer to operate the stack yourself. Appwrite is [open source](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite) and runs anywhere Docker runs.

# What this means for students starting today

If you are enrolling in school now and exploring backend tools, Appwrite is still a strong place to start.

- [Appwrite Cloud](/pricing) has a free plan you can use without a student verification flow.
- Our [docs](/docs) and [quick starts](/docs/quick-starts) walk through auth, databases, storage, functions, and sites.
- You can [self-host](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) for free if Cloud limits are not the right fit.

We are sorry we cannot keep the Education program running as it was. If you are on the Education plan, watch your inbox for transition details before November 1st, 2026. The paths above are how you can keep building on Appwrite after the program ends.

